 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 68,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "EAST: Haworth, the Parsonage and a great many Bront\u00eb tea towels. WEST: Pendle Hill, Clitheroe and the Fylde coast. Mind the sheep, they have right of way."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "A ruined farmhouse on the moor above Haworth, long said to be the model for Wuthering Heights, though the real house was a good deal smaller than the one in the book. The Bront\u00eb children walked these moors all the time. Emily Bront\u00eb's novel came out in 1847, and she died the next year at the parsonage in the village.",
   "plaque": "w55e_top_withens",
   "title": "Top Withens"
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE PENDLE WAY. Forty-five miles in a loop round Pendle Hill, waymarked with a witch on a broomstick. Nobody has asked her to move."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "In 1612 twelve people from the villages round Pendle Hill were accused of witchcraft. One died in Lancaster Castle before her trial, one was tried and hanged at York, and ten were hanged at Lancaster on 20 August 1612. It is one of the best documented witch trials in English history, mainly because the court clerk wrote it all down.",
   "plaque": "w55e_pendle_witches",
   "title": "The Pendle Witches"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "In 1652 the preacher George Fox climbed Pendle Hill and had a vision of a great people to be gathered. That summer he went on to Firbank Fell and Sedbergh and drew crowds. Quakers count it as the beginning of their movement.",
   "plaque": "w55e_george_fox",
   "title": "George Fox on Pendle Hill"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "PENDLE HILL, the Big End. 557 metres of gritstone and heather, standing on its own above the Ribble Valley. On a clear day you can see Blackpool Tower. On most days you can see your own hand."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "Opened in 1816 after more than forty years of building, at 127 miles the longest canal in Britain built as a single waterway. It carried coal east and wool and cotton west, and it still crosses the Pennines. Through Burnley it rides a mile-long embankment above the mill roofs.",
   "plaque": "w55e_leeds_liverpool",
   "title": "The Leeds and Liverpool Canal"
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "WEST: Clitheroe, Preston and the Fylde coast. Blackpool is thirty miles on, give or take a stag party."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "WEAVERS' MILL. Cotton, until the 1960s. Nowadays a craft centre, a cafe and a very large flock of pigeons."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "SUMMIT PATH. The Big End, then back down the same way. Please don't hurry the witches."
  }
 ],
